1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:05,087
This demo is sixteen point nine gigabytes.
That's bigger than most finished games I own.

2
00:00:05,337 --> 00:00:11,913
Seven hundred and seventy megabytes of it is a file they forgot to delete.
And there are three people on this team.

3
00:00:15,763 --> 00:00:24,063
Expedition: Into Darkness is a medieval extraction crawler.
You go down with a torch, you fill your bag, and you try to get back out.

4
00:00:24,313 --> 00:00:32,561
Nine hundred and eleven reviews, sixty nine percent positive. Mixed,
in Steam's words. And about a thousand people are in it right now.

5
00:00:32,811 --> 00:00:39,518
The median reviewer had five hours on it. The top ten percent,
thirty six. One of them, two hundred and thirteen.

6
00:00:39,768 --> 00:00:44,359
So people are staying.
The argument is about everything around the game, not the game.

7
00:00:44,609 --> 00:00:50,166
Steam publishes a file list for every demo,
so let's go through what three people actually shipped.

8
00:00:50,416 --> 00:01:00,101
It's Unity, and the code is C sharp — you can tell because the build has a Game Assembly D L L,
which is what the I L two C P P compiler leaves behind.

9
00:01:00,351 --> 00:01:07,450
That's C sharp translated into C plus plus and compiled native.
You write the easy language and ship the fast one.

10
00:01:07,700 --> 00:01:12,787
Audio is F MOD Studio,
with Google's Resonance Audio next to it for spatial sound.

11
00:01:13,037 --> 00:01:21,938
In a game where the thing about to kill you is behind you in the dark,
that isn't decoration. That's the one middleware I'd argue three people should pay for.

12
00:01:22,188 --> 00:01:33,048
There's also a native plugin in there called R F Lib C Native.
It shows up in other Unity games and I could not work out what it does. If you know, the comments are right there.

13
00:01:33,298 --> 00:01:36,112
Now the size,
because that's the story here.

14
00:01:36,362 --> 00:01:44,009
One scene in the whole build. Twenty three kilobytes.
Everything else streams in from fifteen hundred and fifty five Addressable bundles.

15
00:01:44,259 --> 00:01:49,869
Those bundles are fifteen and a half gigabytes.
The single biggest one is two gigabytes on its own.

16
00:01:50,119 --> 00:01:58,080
Some of that is honest. Look at the crafting screen: every weapon is separate parts,
a shaft and a head, each with its own durability and inertia.

17
00:01:58,330 --> 00:02:07,414
That means a mesh and a texture set for every part, not every weapon.
Armour is the same, piece by piece. Content adds up fast when it's modular.

18
00:02:07,664 --> 00:02:17,819
But three people did not hand-model sixteen gigabytes of medieval kit.
My guess is bought libraries and scanned assets, dropped in at source resolution.

19
00:02:18,069 --> 00:02:24,776
And that's usually what a two gigabyte bundle means.
Not too much art. Art that never got its compression settings looked at.

20
00:02:25,026 --> 00:02:28,076
Then there's the file
that isn't art at all.

21
00:02:28,326 --> 00:02:34,798
Game Assembly dot P D B.
Seven hundred and forty five megabytes of debug symbols, shipped to players.

22
00:02:35,048 --> 00:02:43,061
Symbols are how you read a crash report. They belong on your build server,
not in the download. Nobody has ever needed them to play a game.

23
00:02:43,311 --> 00:02:48,215
With the other four,
that's seven hundred and seventy megabytes of a demo doing nothing.

24
00:02:48,465 --> 00:02:53,787
And the store page says you need eight gigabytes of storage.
The build is sixteen point nine.

25
00:02:54,037 --> 00:02:59,359
None of this is incompetence.
It's what happens when there are three of you and nobody owns the build.

26
00:02:59,609 --> 00:03:02,711
Which shows up in the reviews
exactly where you'd expect.

27
00:03:02,961 --> 00:03:10,269
Performance is the loudest complaint by a distance. Blurry,
poorly optimised, and a volumetric fog you can't turn off.

28
00:03:10,519 --> 00:03:19,133
Co-op, meanwhile, gets a hundred and six mentions in positive reviews and eight in negative ones.
Three people built five player netcode that works.

29
00:03:19,383 --> 00:03:25,228
And people who recommended it had played seven and a half hours.
People who didn't, one.

30
00:03:25,478 --> 00:03:27,692
Here's the whole teardown on one card.

31
00:03:27,942 --> 00:03:40,500
Unity, I L two C P P, so the code is C sharp. Three people. F MOD and Resonance for audio.
And seven hundred and seventy megabytes of debug symbols nobody meant to ship.

32
00:03:40,750 --> 00:03:44,923
Worth a dip,
and it's free while the early access date moves around.

33
00:03:45,173 --> 00:03:52,690
If you're shipping this year, two checkboxes. Strip your symbols.
Then open your own store page and check the number you wrote for storage.

34
00:03:52,940 --> 00:03:59,464
That's my read of it from the outside. If you made this and I got something wrong,
say so in the comments and I'll pin the correction.

35
00:03:59,714 --> 00:04:04,488
Next time, another demo,
another file list. This is Demo Dip.
